  "tutorial_example_entry": {
    "lesson_index": 2,
    "lesson_title": "Cosine Coefficients",
    "lesson_core_move": "Compute the cosine coefficients $a_k$ by integrating a periodic continuous-time signal against each cosine harmonic over one period to measure its contribution to the trigonometric Fourier series.",
    "section_count": 5,
    "sections": [
      {
        "lesson_index": 2,
        "lesson_title": "Cosine Coefficients",
        "section_index": 1,
        "section_title": "Introduction to Cosine Coefficients",
        "step_type": "tutorial",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "tutorial_content": {
          "blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "A trigonometric Fourier series separates a periodic signal into a DC term, cosine harmonics, and sine harmonics. The cosine coefficient $a_k$ measures how much of the $k$th cosine harmonic, $\\cos(k\\omega_0 t)$, is present in $x(t)$ over one period.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "a_k = \\frac{2}{T_0}\\int_{t_0}^{t_0+T_0} x(t)\\cos(k\\omega_0 t)\\,dt",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Read this as a projection: multiply $x(t)$ by the target cosine, integrate over one full period, and scale by $\\frac{2}{T_0}$. Orthogonality makes every nonmatching cosine and every sine term disappear, so the integral isolates only the contribution at index $k$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\int_{t_0}^{t_0+T_0}\\cos(m\\omega_0 t)\\cos(n\\omega_0 t)\\,dt = 0 \\quad (m \\ne n)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "In the matching case, the cosine survives. If $x(t)=A\\cos(k\\omega_0 t)$, then the coefficient formula should return $A$ because the product becomes $\\cos^2(k\\omega_0 t)$ over one period.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\int_{t_0}^{t_0+T_0}\\cos^2(k\\omega_0 t)\\,dt = \\frac{T_0}{2}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "That normalization check confirms the meaning of $a_k$: it is the cosine-harmonic weight of $x(t)$ at harmonic index $k$, recovered by projecting onto $\\cos(k\\omega_0 t)$ over one period.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 2,
        "lesson_title": "Cosine Coefficients",
        "section_index": 2,
        "section_title": "Setting Up the Integral for a Cosine Coefficient",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Given $x(t)=4+3\\cos(\\omega_0 t)-2\\sin(2\\omega_0 t)$ with period $T_0$, write the integral that computes $a_1$ and substitute the signal into it.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is the requested coefficient index $1$, so the matching cosine factor is $\\cos(\\omega_0 t)$. To set up $a_1$, use one full period of the signal and keep the normalization factor $\\frac{2}{T_0}$ in front.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "a_1 = \\frac{2}{T_0}\\int_{t_0}^{t_0+T_0} x(t)\\cos(\\omega_0 t)\\,dt",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Now substitute the given periodic signal directly into the formula. This keeps the setup aligned with the requested harmonic while leaving the integral unevaluated.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "a_1 = \\frac{2}{T_0}\\int_{t_0}^{t_0+T_0} \\bigl(4+3\\cos(\\omega_0 t)-2\\sin(2\\omega_0 t)\\bigr)\\cos(\\omega_0 t)\\,dt",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "This is the full setup for the coefficient calculation: the limits cover one period, the integrand matches the $k=1$ cosine harmonic, and the signal has been inserted without evaluating the integral.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 2,
        "lesson_title": "Cosine Coefficients",
        "section_index": 3,
        "section_title": "Computing a Matching Cosine Harmonic Coefficient",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Given $x(t)=2+5\\cos(3\\omega_0 t)-\\sin(\\omega_0 t)$, compute $a_3$ by integrating over one period and using orthogonality to isolate the matching cosine harmonic.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is the requested coefficient index $3$, so the matching test function is $\\cos(3\\omega_0 t)$. To find $a_3$, project the signal onto that harmonic over one full period.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "a_3 = \\frac{2}{T_0}\\int_{t_0}^{t_0+T_0} x(t)\\cos(3\\omega_0 t)\\,dt",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Substitute the signal and keep the three parts separate: the DC term, the matching cosine term, and the sine term. That makes it clear which contributions orthogonality will eliminate.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "a_3 = \\frac{2}{T_0}\\int_{t_0}^{t_0+T_0} \\bigl(2+5\\cos(3\\omega_0 t)-\\sin(\\omega_0 t)\\bigr)\\cos(3\\omega_0 t)\\,dt",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Over one period, the DC term and the sine term integrate to zero against $\\cos(3\\omega_0 t)$, so only the matching cosine term remains.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "a_3 = \\frac{2}{T_0}\\int_{t_0}^{t_0+T_0} 5\\cos^2(3\\omega_0 t)\\,dt = \\frac{2}{T_0}\\cdot 5 \\cdot \\frac{T_0}{2} = 5",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The coefficient is $5$, which matches the amplitude of the cosine term at the target harmonic. That is exactly what the projection is designed to recover.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 2,
        "lesson_title": "Cosine Coefficients",
        "section_index": 4,
        "section_title": "Computing a Zero Coefficient for a Nonmatching Harmonic",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Given $x(t)=1+4\\cos(2\\omega_0 t)+3\\sin(\\omega_0 t)$, compute $a_3$ and show why every term vanishes when the target cosine harmonic does not appear.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is the requested coefficient index $3$, so the projection uses $\\cos(3\\omega_0 t)$. As before, write the cosine coefficient over one full period with the standard normalization.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "a_3 = \\frac{2}{T_0}\\int_{t_0}^{t_0+T_0} x(t)\\cos(3\\omega_0 t)\\,dt",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Substitute the signal and separate the DC, cosine, and sine parts so each contribution can be checked against the target harmonic.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "a_3 = \\frac{2}{T_0}\\int_{t_0}^{t_0+T_0} \\bigl(1+4\\cos(2\\omega_0 t)+3\\sin(\\omega_0 t)\\bigr)\\cos(3\\omega_0 t)\\,dt",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "No term in $x(t)$ matches $\\cos(3\\omega_0 t)$, so orthogonality kills every product over one period. The DC term is orthogonal to the target cosine, the $\\cos(2\\omega_0 t)$ term is a different cosine harmonic, and the sine term is orthogonal to the cosine test function as well.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "a_3 = \\frac{2}{T_0}\\left(0+0+0\\right)=0",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The coefficient is zero because the signal has no matching cosine harmonic at index $3$. In projection form, the target harmonic is absent, so nothing survives the integral.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 2,
        "lesson_title": "Cosine Coefficients",
        "section_index": 5,
        "section_title": "Computing Cosine Coefficients of a Harmonic Sum",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Given $x(t)=2+3\\cos(\\omega_0 t)-4\\cos(2\\omega_0 t)+5\\cos(4\\omega_0 t)$, compute the ordered cosine coefficients $(a_1,a_2,a_3,a_4)$ by applying linearity and orthogonality term by term.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is the ordered tuple $(a_1,a_2,a_3,a_4)$, so write one projection integral for each requested harmonic. Each coefficient is found by testing $x(t)$ against $\\cos(k\\omega_0 t)$ over one period.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\begin{aligned}\na_1 &= \\frac{2}{T_0}\\int_{t_0}^{t_0+T_0} x(t)\\cos(\\omega_0 t)\\,dt \\\\\na_2 &= \\frac{2}{T_0}\\int_{t_0}^{t_0+T_0} x(t)\\cos(2\\omega_0 t)\\,dt \\\\\na_3 &= \\frac{2}{T_0}\\int_{t_0}^{t_0+T_0} x(t)\\cos(3\\omega_0 t)\\,dt \\\\\na_4 &= \\frac{2}{T_0}\\int_{t_0}^{t_0+T_0} x(t)\\cos(4\\omega_0 t)\\,dt\n\\end{aligned}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Now substitute $x(t)=2+3\\cos(\\omega_0 t)-4\\cos(2\\omega_0 t)+5\\cos(4\\omega_0 t)$ into each integral. Linearity lets the sum split term by term, so each coefficient can be checked against its own cosine test function.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\begin{aligned}\na_1 &= \\frac{2}{T_0}\\int_{t_0}^{t_0+T_0} \\bigl(2+3\\cos(\\omega_0 t)-4\\cos(2\\omega_0 t)+5\\cos(4\\omega_0 t)\\bigr)\\cos(\\omega_0 t)\\,dt \\\\\na_2 &= \\frac{2}{T_0}\\int_{t_0}^{t_0+T_0} \\bigl(2+3\\cos(\\omega_0 t)-4\\cos(2\\omega_0 t)+5\\cos(4\\omega_0 t)\\bigr)\\cos(2\\omega_0 t)\\,dt \\\\\na_3 &= \\frac{2}{T_0}\\int_{t_0}^{t_0+T_0} \\bigl(2+3\\cos(\\omega_0 t)-4\\cos(2\\omega_0 t)+5\\cos(4\\omega_0 t)\\bigr)\\cos(3\\omega_0 t)\\,dt \\\\\na_4 &= \\frac{2}{T_0}\\int_{t_0}^{t_0+T_0} \\bigl(2+3\\cos(\\omega_0 t)-4\\cos(2\\omega_0 t)+5\\cos(4\\omega_0 t)\\bigr)\\cos(4\\omega_0 t)\\,dt\n\\end{aligned}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Orthogonality now does the work. Over one period, the DC term is orthogonal to every cosine, mismatched cosine harmonics vanish, and the matching cosine survives with its amplitude.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "a_1=3,\\qquad a_2=-4,\\qquad a_3=0,\\qquad a_4=5",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The missing $\\cos(3\\omega_0 t)$ term makes $a_3=0$, while the other requested coefficients are read directly from the matching amplitudes. The ordered tuple is the projection result for the whole harmonic sum.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "(a_1,a_2,a_3,a_4)=(3,-4,0,5)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      }
    ]
  },
